## Anti-weakening hostile tests
For every authority-relevant blocker, include cases where:
1. a random well-formed hash is substituted;
2. a different task set is supplied;
3. a provider/model is substituted without a new freeze;
4. evaluator artifact is self-authored by the candidate;
5. missing credentials are represented as success;
6. an unavailable route becomes a scientific zero;
7. a stale CANNOT_CHECK remains after the prerequisite is actually available;
8. a genuine fully bound no-alarm case is blocked forever.

The correct system must reject 1–7 appropriately while allowing 8 to proceed when all obligations are satisfied.

## Resolution loop
For each blocker:
`PRESERVE → CLASSIFY → IDENTIFY EARLIEST MISSING OBLIGATION → PROBE AVAILABILITY → BIND → EXECUTE → VERIFY → RETIRE OLD BLOCKER WITH LINEAGE`

If the prerequisite is still unavailable, update only the checked-at evidence and reopen condition. Do not churn architecture.

## Relationship to recursive closure #315
#315 cannot reach a bounded fixed point while stale or ambiguous CANNOT_CHECK states exist. A genuine external blocker may remain at fixed point, but it must be exact, current, independently inspectable, and automatically reopenable when its prerequisite changes.

## AI-session operating contract
Take one blocker only. Read `AGENTS.md`, #315, the owning issue/protocol, and overlapping PRs. Fetch current `main`. Reproduce the blocker from exact artifacts. Never bypass a gate to make progress. Implement the smallest availability/binding discriminator. Preserve the old CANNOT_CHECK record. Comment exact subject, blocker class, probe result, artifact hashes, and next reopen condition.

