## Why a new experiment is needed

Many current systems have a single preferred self-revision response:

- reflection/self-edit -> change the agent/scaffold;
- world-model revision -> change prediction context/model;
- M-open discovery -> expand the hypothesis/model class;
- goal evolution -> revise the objective;
- evolutionary/meta-agent systems -> search new programs/agents.

But an observed failure does not uniquely identify its remedy. The same residual can arise because evidence is missing, an experiment is non-identifying, parameters are wrong, the model class is incomplete, the representation is inappropriate, the objective is a bad proxy, the method basis is inadequate, execution is broken, or the evaluator itself is defective.

Self-ORION should earn a stronger claim only if it can **discriminate these responsibilities before committing to revision**.

